<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  THE UNIVERSAL ROW. One file, five archetypes.
  32-master-plan-android.md 8.1 / 5.3, 22-components.md 8.1-8.7.
  ============================================================================

  This is the single most repeated object in the product: 137 hand-inlined copies
  collapse into this file. Nothing here is optional decoration — every id below is
  a slot a caller either shows or leaves hidden, and the archetype is decided by
  WHICH ONE TRAILING SLOT is shown, never by a second layout file.

  GEOMETRY — handoff README §6 «Строка», measured off the prototype rather than
  guessed (docs/design2026/port-shots-2/43-routing.geo.json):
      [ 16 gutter ][ 40 tile r12, 22 glyph ][ 16 ][ text column ][ 12 ][ trailing ][ 16 ]
  Text origin 72 = 16 + 40 + 16 on a TILED row; 16 on a PLAIN row (5.3), which is
  what hiding @id/row_tile gives you for free — its end margin goes with it.
  Vertical padding is 10 (from @style/Widget.Departament.Row): 10 + 40 + 10 is the
  prototype's 61dp row.
  minHeight 56, height wrap_content: a two-line subtitle GROWS the row, and at font
  scale 200% every text box grows with it. There is not one fixed height in this file.

  THE TYPE IS §6's, NOT THE CARD'S. Заголовок 14sp/400 (Body) and подпись 12.5sp
  (Caption) — a row title is a LABEL the eye sweeps past, not a heading, and the
  16sp/700 this file used to carry made every settings list shout. RowBinder applies
  both roles on every bind, so the appearances below are the resting state a
  tools-preview shows and never the last word.

  THE TWO ROW SPECIES ARE DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN (5.3): a group is tiled if and only if
  its rows carry a leading glyph that DIFFERS between rows. If every row would carry
  the same glyph, the group is plain and @id/row_tile stays gone. N identical tiles
  down the left edge is the generated-settings tell, not a design.

  EVERYTHING OPTIONAL SHIPS GONE. A caller shows exactly what its archetype needs,
  so a forgotten line is a missing element and never a wrong one:

    Row.Navigation   row_title [+ row_subtitle] [+ row_value] + row_chevron
    Row.Value        row_title + row_value + row_chevron (or row_trailing_glyph to
                     cycle the value in place, which promises no screen and no dialog)
    Row.Toggle       row_title [+ row_subtitle] + row_switch
    Row.Action       row_title [+ row_subtitle] + row_icon_action OR row_action
                     AND the row itself stops being a target (see below)
    Row.Destructive  row_title in TextAppearance.App.Title.Destructive, no trailing
    Row.Fact         row_title + row_value, not clickable
    Row.Selectable   row_title [+ row_value] + row_marker, view.isActivated = true

  THE ONE SANCTIONED PAIRING (8.1): a row may carry a VALUE plus one trailing
  affordance — value + chevron (22-components 8.3) or value + state marker (the
  server row: ping plus check). The value is text, not a control. Two CONTROLS in
  the trailing slot never happens: never a chevron and a switch, never two glyphs.

  THE BINDER IS THE FRONT DOOR. ui/component/RowBinder.bind(root, title, …, trailing)
  resolves every slot below by name, shows exactly one trailing affordance, resets the
  others, and refuses at the call site to give a row two targets. Bind through it
  rather than reaching for these ids one at a time.

  WHAT CANNOT BE A LAYOUT ATTRIBUTE, AND WHY. A Material style binds at inflation, so
  three archetype deltas are applied to the inflated row instead. Each is an exact
  reproduction of a ratified style, not an invented value, and RowBinder already does
  all three for you:
    Row.Action       row.isClickable = false; row.isFocusable = false
                     row.setBackgroundResource(0)      <- @style/…Row.Action verbatim
    Row.Destructive  title.setTextAppearance(R.style.TextAppearance_App_Title_Destructive)
    accent tile      rowTile.setBackgroundResource(R.drawable.bg_tile_accent) + the
                     matching glyph tint  (D-5: one accent tile per screen, at most)
  Disabled is alpha 0.38 on the row plus isEnabled = false (R6). Pressed, focused and
  selected are already drawn by @drawable/bg_row and need no code at all.

  ACCESSIBILITY. The WHOLE ROW is the touch target — that is why the switch below is
  explicitly not clickable and not focusable (five shipped switch rows have two
  targets that can disagree). The tile is decorative and is marked as such: the title
  is the row's name. A selected row must announce it (isSelected on the a11y node),
  not only draw it.
-->
<!-- clipToPadding=false IS THE PRESS PLATE'S FIX, and it is the whole of «выделил зажав на
     три точки … обрезается».

     The row's paddingEnd is the 16dp gutter, and RowBinder.alignInkToGutter parks the
     trailing slot INSIDE that padding with a negative end margin so the glyph's ink can
     reach the line. A ViewGroup clips its children to its padding box by default, so that
     padding was also a CLIP LINE 16dp inside the card: the «⋮» button sat flush with the
     card's edge, its 28dp press disc ran from 4 to 32dp inside it, and everything past 16
     was cut off flat. Twelve of the disc's twenty-eight dp, on the side the finger is on —
     which is why a round plate came back looking shorn no matter what the inset was set to.
     Rendered and measured: plate painted 296…312 in a 328dp card, i.e. stopping exactly on
     the 16dp padding line rather than on its own 324.

     Nothing here moves as a result — the row draws its background full-bleed and lays every
     child out inside the padding as before. What changes is that a child DELIBERATELY placed
     in the padding is no longer amputated by it. RowBinder.pullInk's «nothing clips» is true
     now; it was not before. -->
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    android:id="@+id/row"
    style="@style/Widget.Departament.Row"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:baselineAligned="false"
    android:clipToPadding="false">

    <!-- TILED groups only. Gone = a plain row at the 16dp origin (5.3). The glyph is
         the caller's; the 40/12/22 geometry and the neutral fill are the style's. -->
    <ImageView
        android:id="@+id/row_tile"
        style="@style/Widget.Departament.Tile"
        android:backgroundTint="?attr/colorSurfaceContainerHighest"
        android:layout_width="@dimen/tile_size"
        android:layout_height="@dimen/tile_size"
        android:layout_marginEnd="@dimen/space_16"
        android:importantForAccessibility="no"
        android:visibility="gone"
        tools:src="@drawable/ic_settings_24dp"
        tools:visibility="visible" />

    <!-- The text column. weight 1, so a long title yields to the trailing slot and a
         long value never pushes the title out of the row. -->
    <LinearLayout
        android:id="@+id/row_text"
        android:layout_width="0dp"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_weight="1"
        android:orientation="vertical">

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/row_title"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:ellipsize="end"
            android:maxLines="2"
            android:textAppearance="@style/TextAppearance.App.Body"
            tools:text="Маршрутизация" />

        <!-- A subtitle that restates its title is deleted, not written (8.1). -->
        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/row_subtitle"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/space_4"
            android:ellipsize="end"
            android:maxLines="2"
            android:textAppearance="@style/TextAppearance.App.Caption"
            android:visibility="gone"
            tools:text="Правила для доменов и приложений"
            tools:visibility="visible" />
    </LinearLayout>

    <!-- THE TRAILING SLOT. Show exactly one control. row_value is text and may
         accompany row_chevron or row_marker; it is not a control.

         THE GAP IS NOT UNIFORM, and the difference is the handoff's, not a rounding
         error. §6: «Правый отступ 12dp только когда справа есть значение или
         переключатель» — the prototype writes it as
         `gap: (isToggle || pop || value) ? 12 : 0`. So a VALUE, a SWITCH or a BUTTON
         takes 12 from the text column, because each of those is a second thing to read
         and it needs the air. A bare GLYPH — chevron, caret, state marker — takes 4:
         it is punctuation on the row, not a second column, and 12 in front of it opens
         a gutter down the right edge of a card that has no value anywhere in it.

         WHAT THE SLOT ENDS ON IS THE 16dp GUTTER, AND FOR A GLYPH THAT MEANS ITS INK.
         Everything else on the row is measured to that line — the text column, the
         card's content edge, the screen's — so a trailing element that stops 6dp short
         of it is adrift even when its box is perfectly placed: «также кнопки не
         доделал… надо их сделать нормально правее». An object with a boundary of its
         own (row_check_box's bordered box, row_switch's track, row_action's button)
         aligns by that boundary and takes no correction; a bare glyph has no boundary,
         so its last painted pixel IS its edge and it carries a negative end margin
         worth exactly its own empty air. Five glyphs, five numbers, all measured off
         the drawables in values/tokens_row_action.xml. -->

    <!-- 12dp: a value is text to be read, so it gets the full gap. -->


    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/row_value"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/space_12"
        android:ellipsize="end"
        android:gravity="end"
        android:maxLines="1"
        android:textAppearance="@style/TextAppearance.App.Subtitle"
        android:visibility="gone"
        tools:text="Cloudflare"
        tools:visibility="visible" />

    <!-- State marker: this item is the current selection. Not an action (8.1). The slot
         is ALWAYS reserved and selection moves its alpha, so choosing an item never
         reflows the list (22-components 18.1).

         THE END MARGIN IS NEGATIVE, and every glyph below carries one too. The box
         already ends on the row's 16dp padding; what the eye lines a column up by is
         the last painted PIXEL, and ic_action_done leaves 2.25dp of its viewport empty
         on that side, so without the pull the tick stops 18.25dp from the edge while
         the chevron in the next card stops at 22 and the text column ends at 16. The
         number is this glyph's own air and nothing else — the arithmetic for all five
         is on values/tokens_row_action.xml. It is written HERE, next to the `src` it
         was measured from, because this slot's drawable is declared in this file:
         nothing at runtime can put a different glyph in it and inherit a pull that
         does not fit. -->
    <ImageView
        android:id="@+id/row_marker"
        android:layout_width="@dimen/glyph_18"
        android:layout_height="@dimen/glyph_18"
        android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/space_4"
        android:layout_marginEnd="@dimen/row_glyph_nudge_check"
        android:importantForAccessibility="no"
        android:src="@drawable/ic_action_done"
        android:tint="?attr/colorPrimary"
        android:visibility="gone"
        tools:visibility="visible" />

    <!-- Tapping pushes a screen. Swap the src for the expand or cycle glyph when the
         promise changes (8.1); the size and the tone stay.

         THE BIGGEST PULL OF THE FIVE, 6dp: ic_chevron_right is one stroke drawn in a
         viewport that also has to hold the arrow's other three sides, so a quarter of
         its width on the trailing side is empty. This is the glyph the product has
         most of and the one that made the misalignment invisible for two rounds —
         with nothing beside it to be compared with, a chevron 6dp inside the gutter
         just looks like the gutter.

         RowBinder.Trailing.Expand ROTATES this view 90°, which swings the ink's own
         edges: the caret then reaches 3.45dp inside the box instead of 6, i.e. 2.55dp
         past the gutter. It is left alone on purpose. The resting angle is what a
         column of rows is read by, the rotation is a draw-time transform, and chasing
         it would mean writing a layout parameter on every frame of a 220ms
         animation — a reflow to fix a tenth of a millimetre. -->
    <ImageView
        android:id="@+id/row_chevron"
        android:layout_width="@dimen/glyph_18"
        android:layout_height="@dimen/glyph_18"
        android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/space_4"
        android:layout_marginEnd="@dimen/row_glyph_nudge_chevron"
        android:importantForAccessibility="no"
        android:src="@drawable/ic_chevron_right"
        android:tint="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant"
        android:visibility="gone"
        tools:visibility="visible" />

    <!-- Tapping changes the value in place: no screen, no dialog. The caller sets the
         glyph (the cycle affordance wants ic_unfold_more, which res/drawable does not
         carry yet), so there is no src here to be wrong.

         AND THEREFORE NO END MARGIN HERE EITHER. The two slots above pull themselves
         because this file declares what is in them; this one is filled by
         RowBinder.Trailing.Glyph, so its pull is applied on every bind by
         RowBinder.alignInkToGutter, which knows the glyph. A glyph that function has
         never seen gets NO pull — the slot then draws exactly where it drew before
         any of this existed, which is the one safe answer to an icon nobody has
         measured. -->
    <ImageView
        android:id="@+id/row_trailing_glyph"
        android:layout_width="@dimen/glyph_18"
        android:layout_height="@dimen/glyph_18"
        android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/space_4"
        android:importantForAccessibility="no"
        android:tint="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant"
        android:visibility="gone"
        tools:src="@drawable/ic_arrow_drop_down" />

    <!-- MEMBERSHIP OF A SET, not a boolean applied now (§7's «список приложений с
         чекбоксами»). 22dp box, 2dp border, radius 7, a 16dp tick that exists only
         when the box is filled — @drawable/bg_checkbox and @color/checkbox_tick do
         both halves off one state, so the row cannot draw a filled box with no tick.
         Not focusable and not clickable for the same reason the switch is not: the
         WHOLE row is the target. -->
    <ImageView
        android:id="@+id/row_check_box"
        android:layout_width="@dimen/checkbox_size"
        android:layout_height="@dimen/checkbox_size"
        android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/space_12"
        android:background="@drawable/bg_checkbox"
        android:importantForAccessibility="no"
        android:padding="@dimen/space_2"
        android:scaleType="fitCenter"
        android:src="@drawable/ic_action_done"
        android:tint="@color/checkbox_tick"
        android:visibility="gone"
        tools:visibility="visible" />

    <!-- The row owns the toggle; the switch is decoration that reports state.

         AND clickable="false" HAS A SECOND EFFECT, which cost the owner a screenshot
         of the IPv6 row: ViewGroup.dispatchSetPressed skips a child that can be
         pressed on its own and forwards the state to every child that cannot, so the
         flag that makes the row the single hit target is precisely what feeds the
         row's press into this switch. M3's thumb answered with a 28dp disc. Do NOT
         "fix" that by making the switch clickable — that trades one defect for two
         targets. It is refused at @drawable/switch_thumb, which is Material's thumb
         with the pressed entry left out and every other state reproduced. -->
    <com.google.android.material.materialswitch.MaterialSwitch
        android:id="@+id/row_switch"
        style="@style/Widget.Departament.Switch"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/space_12"
        android:clickable="false"
        android:focusable="false"
        android:importantForAccessibility="no"
        android:visibility="gone"
        tools:visibility="visible" />

    <!-- Row.Action's in-place action. When this is shown the ROW is not a target:
         two targets doing different things is the layout_subscription_meta_bar
         mistake. android:contentDescription is MANDATORY and states the ACTION.

         IT IS THE ONE TRAILING SLOT THAT IS SIZED HERE, and the size is the fix.
         @style/Widget.Departament.Button.Icon is a 48dp touch minimum holding a 22dp
         icon, so 13dp of the box is air before the glyph starts and its ink lands 10
         to 16dp further in than a chevron's — «они улетели куда-то влево». One round
         answered that by pulling the box, which works until the target is the gutter:
         «⋮» would need -22.17 and the last 6.17 of it hangs outside the row, where
         the box is clipped and the press plate is shorn. So the box is laid out at
         @dimen/row_action_size instead — 36, the size the подписка card's action
         discs draw at — and the 48dp target is restored by RowBinder with the same
         TouchDelegate those discs use. Drawn size and touched size were never obliged
         to be the same number; 00-rules.md 14.2 is about the second one.

         An explicit width and height also retire the style's 48dp minWidth/minHeight
         for this instance (an EXACTLY spec ignores them), which is what stops the
         touch minimum from setting the ROW's height: «Версия» и «Идентификатор»
         stood 8dp taller than the tiled rows under them for no reason a reader could
         see.

         THE PADDING'S ONLY JOB IS TO BE SYMMETRIC, and the note that used to stand
         here had the mechanism wrong twice over. A MaterialButton's iconGravity is
         `textStart` (2) — Widget.Material3.Button sets it, the library's own default
         `start` never reaches this chain — and textStart CENTRES the glyph rather than
         parking it at paddingLeft:

             iconLeft   = (W − textWidth − paddingEnd − iconSize − iconPadding − paddingStart) / 2
             drawn left = paddingLeft + iconLeft = (W − iconSize) / 2 + (paddingLeft − paddingRight) / 2

         Measured on the inflated row: box 36, resolved padding 11/11, icon bounds
         Rect(−4, 0, 18, 22), so the glyph draws at 11 − 4 = 7 and is dead centre. The
         VALUE of @dimen/row_action_glyph_air never mattered; only that the two sides
         are equal. They are, so the glyph is centred and the plate below is concentric
         with it — the one invariant this slot has to keep.

         (Eleven and not seven because MaterialButtonHelper.loadFromAttributes ends with
         setPaddingRelative(paddingStart + insetLeft, …): every inset is ADDED to the
         padding as well as taken off the background. Symmetric insets keep symmetric
         padding, which is why the four below are one number and not two.)

         AND THE PLATE IS INSET SO IT IS SMALLER THAN THE BOX. Once «⋮»'s ink is on the
         gutter its 36dp box is FLUSH with the row's end edge, and a plate that filled
         the box would end on the card's own clip line. @dimen/row_action_plate_inset
         leaves a 28dp disc, concentric with the glyph, 4dp clear of the card on the
         worst glyph and 10.25 on «копировать». Four insets and not two — a
         horizontal-only inset makes the drawable 36×28 and GradientDrawable then clamps
         the pill to half the shorter side, i.e. a stadium where the design says disc.

         THAT DISC WAS STILL BEING CUT, and not by the card: by this row's own
         paddingEnd, which is a clip line until clipToPadding is turned off. See the
         note above the root.

         ITS END MARGIN IS STILL NOT ZERO AT RUNTIME: 36 leaves 7dp of air beside the
         glyph and each glyph leaves its own, so RowBinder.alignInkToGutter pulls per
         glyph on every bind. The correction is not written here because the icon is
         the caller's. -->
    <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
        android:id="@+id/row_icon_action"
        style="@style/Widget.Departament.Button.Icon"
        android:layout_width="@dimen/row_action_size"
        android:layout_height="@dimen/row_action_size"
        android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/space_12"
        android:insetBottom="@dimen/row_action_plate_inset"
        android:insetLeft="@dimen/row_action_plate_inset"
        android:insetRight="@dimen/row_action_plate_inset"
        android:insetTop="@dimen/row_action_plate_inset"
        android:paddingStart="@dimen/row_action_glyph_air"
        android:paddingTop="@dimen/row_action_glyph_air"
        android:paddingEnd="@dimen/row_action_glyph_air"
        android:paddingBottom="@dimen/row_action_glyph_air"
        android:visibility="gone"
        app:icon="@drawable/ic_copy"
        tools:ignore="ContentDescription"
        tools:visibility="visible" />

    <!-- The other half of Row.Action: a Tertiary button, and this row is the ONLY place
         in the product a Tertiary is allowed to live inside a row («Привязать Telegram»
         -> «Привязать»). The row is inert whenever this is showing. -->
    <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
        android:id="@+id/row_action"
        style="@style/Widget.Departament.Button.Tertiary"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/space_12"
        android:visibility="gone"
        tools:text="Привязать"
        tools:visibility="visible" />
</LinearLayout>
